"[2601.09833v1] Stable and Explainable Personality Trait Evaluation in Large Language Models with Internal Activations (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09833v1)\nciteturn27124view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09833v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 14 Jan 2026]\nL9: # Title:Stable and Explainable Personality Trait Evaluation in Large Language Models with Internal Activations\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Xiaoxu Ma , cite6†Xiangbo Zhang , cite7†Zhenyu Weng L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Stable and Explainable Personality Trait Evaluation in Large Language Models with Internal Activations, by Xiaoxu Ma and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Evaluating personality traits in Large Language Models (LLMs) is key to model interpretation, comparison, and responsible deployment. However, existing questionnaire-based evaluation methods exhibit limited stability and offer little explainability, as their results are highly sensitive to minor variations in prompt phrasing or role-play configurations.\nL17: To address these limitations, we propose an internal-activation-based approach, termed Persona-Vector Neutrality Interpolation (PVNI), for stable and explainable personality trait evaluation in LLMs. PVNI extracts a persona vector associated with a target personality trait from the model's internal activations using contrastive prompts.\nL18: It then estimates the corresponding neutral score by interpolating along the persona vector as an anchor axis, enabling an interpretable comparison between the neutral prompt representation and the persona direction. We provide a theoretical analysis of the effectiveness and generalization properties of PVNI. Extensive experiments across diverse LLMs demonstrate that PVNI yields substantially more stable personality trait evaluations than existing methods, even under questionnaire and role-play variants.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2601.09833 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2601.09833v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.09833†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Xiaoxu Ma [cite13†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Wed, 14 Jan 2026 19:48:59 UTC (2,791 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Stable and Explainable Personality Trait Evaluation in Large Language Models with Internal Activations, by Xiaoxu Ma and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite8†View PDF L35:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite14†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L44: \nL45: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2026-01 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite21†cs L50: \nL51: ### References & Citations\nL52: \nL53:   * cite22†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L54:   * cite23†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L55:   * cite24†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L56: \nL57: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL58: \nL59: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL60: \nL61: [Button: ×]\nL62: \nL63: loading...\nL64: \nL65: Data provided by:\nL66: \nL67: ### Bookmark\nL68: \nL69: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL70: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL73: \nL74: Bibliographic Explorer (cite25†What is the Explor